3 STAE New Mexico Territory
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 7 OCT 1827
2 ADDR
3 CITY Llano Quemado
3 STAE Nuevo México
3 CTRY Mexico
1 OCCU Farmer/Rancher
1 NOTE {geni:about_me}     Francisco was born at Llano Quemado (near Ranchos de Taos), Taos County, New Mexico.  He married Feliciana Cordova from Ranchos de Taos in Taos in 1849. By 1850 he and his bride are living in Llano Largo (Penas
2 CONC co area), as are his sibblings and parents.  He was living back in ancestral lands of Llano Largo when she passed away two days after giving birth to their eighth child, Tio Zacarias in 1859.  He remarried in four years later to G
2 CONC uillerma Garcia from Ranchos de Taos and eventually He moved to Arroyo Seco. 
2 CONT
2 CONT      Francisco was living in Los Pinos, NM when he enlisted in the New Mexico Volunteers during the Civil War.  He enlisted at Fort Garland on October 30, 1862, CO for three years.  He fought at the battles of Valverde and Gloriet
2 CONC a and rose to the rank of Corporal.  He was a scout and escort for Col. Kit Carson and was involved in removing the Navajos from Canon de Chelly to the Bosque Redondo near Fort Sumner.
2 CONT
2 CONT      The ruins (noticible mounds) of his home in Arroyo Seco are located directly east of where Melquiades and Candelaria Madrid used to live.  Bounded on the west side by the Madrid property and on the east by the property that u
2 CONC sed to belong to Tio Saturnino Martinez then later to Luciano Garcia, and now to Edelman, and directly behind the house that used to belong to cousin Flavio Martinez.  It is believed that Francisco had a mill next to the Arroyo Se
2 CONC co river.  When Mr. Edelman made his pond by the river, two mill stones were found.  At an earlier time a local young man who ran off the road and into the river in his car, found at that same site a smaller white stone mill when 
2 CONC he crawled out of the car.
2 CONT
2 CONT     He died at a dance from a heart attack in the village of Arroyo Seco, NM., and is burried in Ranchos Taos, just to the right of the entrance to the Saint Francis Catholic church  
