1 NAME Bonifacio Geronimo /Luján/
2 GIVN Bonifacio Geronimo
2 SURN Luján
2 _MARNM Luján
2 NICK Bonnie Geromino Luján
1 SEX M
1 GRAD
2 PLAC Clayton High School
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 5 JUN 1901
2 ADDR
3 STAE Oklahoma Territory
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 12 MAY 1983
2 ADDR
3 CITY Clayton
3 STAE New Mexico
3 CTRY United States
1 BURI
2 DATE 14 MAY 1983
2 ADDR
3 CITY Clayton
3 STAE New Mexico
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Manager of the Townsman Motel
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} (This is written by Amy Hudson Johnson-- Some of my memories--- Please add yours, too!!)   
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandpa Bonnie was very much beloved by all of us who knew him!  He was always busy going and doing, (Mom and I call it puttering), and usually whistling or humming a happy tune!  He loved teaching us songs that he knew: "My Bonni
2 CONC e lies over the Ocean", (which I thought was silly 'cause MY Bonnie was "over here"!)  Another; "I went to the animal fair; the birds and the bees were there; the old racoon by the light of the moon was combing his auburn hair. . 
2 CONC . The monkey, he got drunk, and fell on the elephant's trunk, the elephant sneezed and fell on his knees, and what became of the MONK, the monk, the monk???" 
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandpa had a passion for trying to teach us Spanish phrases, which I didn't appreciate at the time, but I sure still remember all of them to this day, (and have attempted to expand my Spanish at various times.)  I think he would 
2 CONC be very pleased if he knew how much I appreciate it now!!
2 CONT
2 CONT I remember often Grandpa would sear his green chile peppers over the gas burners in the kitchen, and I would hate the smell and the fumes!!  Now we sear our own home-grown chile peppers on a chosen day in the fall, so tears come t
2 CONC o my eyes for sweet and dear cherished memories, and the fumes are intoxicating and wonderful!!  Grandpa would pour his red chile flakes over everything on his plate, and the sweat would pour down his head as he ate! 
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandpa imparted his love of animals and his gift of training animals to me!   I remember him running his beloved Penny and Rascal through all their tricks, and little did he know, he was training me, as well!  I discovered I had 
2 CONC the same gift with animals as he did!!  (My Dad calls me "Ellie May", as I have always loved my 'critters'.)  I can almost see Grandpa as a boy down on the Carrumpa running across the prairie with a beloved dog at his side searchi
2 CONC ng for high adventure!!  (I am so there, baby!!)  I also learned to whistle the call of the meadow lark from Grandpa when we would go "out to the back 40," behind the motel. (The whistle of our local lark is subtley different; he 
2 CONC doesn't know what to think of his New Mexico cousin's call!!) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandpa could be quite stern at times, but He taught us a GREAT deal about respecting one's elders, and minding one's manners!  (This is VASTLY lacking in our casual world of today, que no?)  I am sure he excercised great leniency
2 CONC  when it came to his grand-children, for he came from the school of "not speaking unless spoken to," etc.  But He was also quite affectionate, and would let us "pound" on his belly like it was a drum!!  As with ALL the Lujan men, 
2 CONC I thought he was quite handsome, and had a fine sense of humor!!
2 CONT
2 CONT I remember Grandpa (Bonnie) Lujan was a very reverent and religious man.  I saw him on his knees every night at his bed-side praying and reciting his rosary.  (I may now surmise he was interceding for one son in Viet Nam, another 
2 CONC son traveling all over the world, his  daughters, and all his grandchildren, etc., etc.)  Later in life, Grandma (Dottie) told us he very much enjoyed watching Billy Graham and other pastors on TV.  The Bible tells us  religion is
2 CONC  of no value unless it is mixed with faith, and I am convinced Grandpa was a man of great faith!!
2 CONT
2 CONT I also remember the smell of the inside of Grandpa's (195?)  black and white Chevy Bel Air!!  And also the smell of "the little house out back."  Not the old chicken coop, but the little shed in the back yard.  It smelled like mot
2 CONC or oil and had old pictures and license plates nailed to the walls. We would crawl through the doggy-door to slip in whenever we wanted, and it was cool and dark in there on a hot summer day.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandpa was always telling us "Cuidado",  ("Be careful!')  for everything!  And he used to call me "Amos Rooneeay."  Say it fast.  (for Renee')   That used to bother me a lot, but there was no doubt in my mind that my Grandpa will
2 CONC  always love this little "hijita". (Pardon my redundancy!!)   I will always love you, too, my little Abuelito!!                
1 NOTE {geni:hair_color} Black
1 NOTE {geni:eye_color} Brown
1 NOTE {geni:height_1} 5 feet
1 NOTE {geni:height_2} 4 inches
