1 NOTE {geni:about_me} "On Savino Jose Chavis genology all that is know by mother Adelia Hern Chavis his wife is that Savino Jose Chavis was born in 1875 Aug 17 somewhere near La Loma Colo. And Savino Jose Chavis father was also born in 
2 CONC the San Louis Valley in Colo. His name was Jose Guadalupe [Guadalupe]Chavis it is also known that his father was a Coloradian." notations in 1953 journal by Adelia Hern's daughter
2 CONT
2 CONT Jose Guadalupe Chavez would have been born in what was then the Rio Arriba Co. of NM but what later, after 1861, became Conejos Co CO. His birth place on the 1860 census of Conejos Co. states he was born in NM but he hadn't moved 
2 CONC from his present location of what became Conejos Co. CO. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/p/a/y/Mae-M-Payne/WEBSITE-0001/UHP-0005.html
2 CONT
2 CONT     The small village of La Loma de San José consisted of a long, straight house building (casita) composed of 17 individual rooms.  Each room  housed one family.   Wall construction used 14 inch adobe bricks without stone foundat
2 CONC ions.  Vigas and latillas provided traditional roofing materials.  River silt mixed with sheep or goat blood packed into a glossed, durable floor.  Corner style fireplaces provided heat and cooking fires for each living unit. Two 
2 CONC rooms within the long casita probably  served as a trading post and storeroom separated by an interior log wall.
2 CONT
2 CONT     The village adhered to a cordilleran (corridor) settlement plan.  East of the casita, the founders constructed a small log and mud chapel (jacal).  Technically, the terms jacal and morada  indicate construction methods.  Withi
2 CONC n the San Luis Valley, jacal (built with vertical logs) also identifies a regular chapel while morada (built with horizontal logs) identifies a Penitente  structure. A wide lane with three circles of stones marking locations of tr
2 CONC ansplanted trees separated the east and west structures.   Corrals, stock sheds, and kitchen middens marked the southeast corner of the village.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Farther up the hillside  stood the cemetery.  Grave markers include basalt boulders, locally quarried volcanic tuff, and simple wooden crosses.  An adobe wall surrounded the cemetery.  An opening faced  north toward the villag
2 CONC e.  The national register of historic cemeteries includes La Loma de San José.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://museumtrail.org/lalomadesanjose/site&map.htm
