1 NAME Jose Epifanio /Torres/
2 GIVN Jose Epifanio
2 SURN Torres
2 _MARNM Chavez
2 NICK Epifanio
1 NAME Jose Epifanio Torres Chavez
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 30 OCT 1894
2 PLAC Torres Family Farm
2 ADDR
3 CITY Las Palomas
3 STAE New Mexico
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 29 DEC 1976
2 ADDR
3 CITY Montebello
3 STAE California
3 CTRY United States
1 BURI
2 DATE ABT 6 JAN 1977
2 ADDR
3 CITY Las Palomas
3 STAE New Mexico
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU retired farmer, builder, construction
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://sites.rootsweb.com/%7Enmscgs/SOCORRO/wga109.html#I26526
2 CONT
2 CONT https://sites.rootsweb.com/%7Enmscgs/SOCORRO/wga109.html#I26526
2 CONT
2 CONT Jose Epifanio was born in 1894 at the family farms at Las Palomas, Sierra County, New Mexico. At that time that farmland had many members of the Torres and extended families busily carving out an honourable living for all those wi
2 CONC lling to give it all their time and effort. At least 3 generations of Torres occupied those farms. He was the second child born to Prajedes and Ninfa Torres in October 30, 1894, was able to attend school right through to 3rd grade
2 CONC  level then contributed his efforts to the chores of maintaining the farm with his parents, grandparents, siblings and other relatives living on the farmlands,  married Ocaris Coronado Barreras from neighbouring community of Cuchi
2 CONC llo on December 28, 1928 at his age of 34 and hers at 21.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jose Epifanio were successful in their efforts to maintain the farms up until 1951 and raise 11 children, 8 boys and 3 girls to become fully educated at least the high school graduate level. In the year 1951 when for the second co
2 CONC nsecutive year all crops were lost and entire farmland destroyed for future farming in the near term due to the arroyo flooding all farmlands belonging to the Torres tribe and most other families adjacent to the arroyo. Everybody 
2 CONC affected badly moved away. Some relocated 15 miles away at Hot Springs, New Mexico and others spread far and wide, mostly to California.
2 CONT
2 CONT By this time several children were old enough to help earn a living with different jobs or moved out for homes of their own or enlisted in the military. Even so times were very hard financially and saw great sorrow in the lost way
2 CONC  of living. Jose Epifanio was able to find work at various construction and carpentry sites in the area and sometimes away. His most steady job was as a grounds and building maintenance labourer for Carrie Tingley Childrens Hospit
2 CONC al in town. He loved working for the children, but did not ever like the actual work itself.
2 CONT
2 CONT He worked hard well into his mid 70's and unfortunately lost his youngest son, Juan Jose when Juan was only 24 in November of 1971 from complications of the dreaded epilepsy which he developed very early in his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jose Epifanio lived to see that his children having survived the hardships of the 50's were well situated with their own families and was there to meet and enjoy several of his earliest grandchildren starting in 1957 and reached t
2 CONC he age of 82 before succumbing to his respiratory ailments in December 29, 1976. In his final few years he enjoyed living with various sons and daughters in New Mexico and California. He and his beloved wife were never separated f
2 CONC rom living together.
