3 STAE NM
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 25 SEP 1965
2 ADDR
3 CITY Medford
3 STAE OR
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} What I knew of my grandfather [M. Michael Payne] was that he had a wooden leg, he was a big man and he loved to tease. My father thought he'd lost his leg in a mining accident, he told me he lost in a train acciden
2 CONC t when I was little and later he said it was a construciton accident. 
2 CONT
2 CONT My grandfather was retired from heavy construction when I knew him, and had been in mining when his children were small. My father remembers him in Cimarron breaking horses, and working on a ranch there. He is also remembered by h
2 CONC is daughters as, for a time, being a bootlegger but that was a short-lived experience as he came home one night thoroughly scared and never made a run again. Someone, or something scared him pretty good. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandfather also owned land in Colorado near and a house in Montrose. My father remembers the house in Montrose that they lived in for a time. They were living in Cimarron when my father was born, the youngest boy of two. He had 5
2 CONC  sisters, and two younger than himself. They moved to Medford OR when my dad was in high school. My grandfather was never involved in military service that I know of yet my Uncle was in the WWII and my father in Korea. 
2 CONT
2 CONT My grandparents were Protestant, and had always been. My great grandparents were Protestant. My Great Grandmother Adelia was Protestant and GGrandfather Savino grew up Catholic but changed when when his wife challenged him to read
2 CONC  a Bible and find out for himself what it said. Savino freely changed to Protestant and raised his kids in that faith. But most of the rest of the family was Catholic.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, all of Edward Chavis' children were Protestant. Not a Catholic in the batch anywhere.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/p/a/y/Mae-M-Payne/WEBSITE-0001/UHP-0002.html
